WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.044 --> 00:00:04.338
- Contact, bearing 0-9-1,
range indefinite.

00:00:04.421 --> 00:00:05.839
- Most likely a U-boat, sir.

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:10.385
- "The Battle of the Atlantic"
is everything that happened

00:00:10.469 --> 00:00:13.722
in the Atlantic ocean
from the moment War World II began.

00:00:15.307 --> 00:00:18.936
There were thousands of ships that were
operating at any given moment,

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:23.357
trying to make the perilous journey from
the Western Hemisphere to Britain

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.442
sending what was needed
to liberate Europe.

00:00:25.526 --> 00:00:26.985
- U-boats, starboard bow!

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:33.784
- And they were all would-be targets
of the Nazi submarines.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.205
- Halfdof reports
a German transmission

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:41.542
bearing 0-8-7 degrees,
range 15 to 20 miles.

00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:44.753
- These convoys would have to
make long zig-zagging courses

00:00:44.837 --> 00:00:48.590
hopefully avoiding the submarines,
but they were reduced to the speed

00:00:48.674 --> 00:00:51.051
of the slowest ship in the convoy.

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:55.556
And the Nazi submarines were just as fast
when they were on the surface.

00:00:55.639 --> 00:00:57.224
- Conn, Combat.
Looks like our friends

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.685
are coming up for fresh air and
to maintain speed with us.

00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:01.812
Contact bearing 2-0-7.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.188
Range: three miles.

00:01:05.566 --> 00:01:08.819
- Early in the war,
the American ships had radar.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:10.779
They could see what
was on the surface.

00:01:10.863 --> 00:01:14.408
But it was a spotty radar.
It often didn't work very well.

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:15.742
- What's going on?
- I don't know, sir.

00:01:15.826 --> 00:01:17.160
I can't get a good read.

00:01:17.244 --> 00:01:19.454
- So you're relying on your eyes...

00:01:19.538 --> 00:01:22.207
much like a guy
with binoculars on the bridge.

00:01:22.291 --> 00:01:25.919
With sonar and hydrophones,
relying on your ears.

00:01:26.003 --> 00:01:28.338
- Got 'em. Contact close.
Starboard beam, 0-8-3.

00:01:28.422 --> 00:01:30.424
- Now, Mr. Lopez!
- Fire, fire.

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:35.846
- Come on.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.807
- And all those things can play
tricks with you over time.

00:01:38.891 --> 00:01:40.684
- Sonar, read anything further?

00:01:46.148 --> 00:01:48.275
- Still stationary. Possible decoy.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:52.154
- Sonar reports contact is
not moving, possible decoy.

00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:57.451
- Yet another challenge
was how primitive

00:01:57.534 --> 00:02:00.495
communication was between parts
of the ship to the captain.

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:02.122
- Contact bearings.

00:02:02.206 --> 00:02:05.042
- Contact read starboard 0-1-2,
range 600 yards sir.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:06.668
- Mr. Lopez, stand by with
a medium pattern.

00:02:06.752 --> 00:02:07.920
- I'm ready, sir.

00:02:08.003 --> 00:02:12.758
- It was a guy's job to repeat
what they'd just heard

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:16.762
to the captain, or repeat
what the captain just told them,

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:20.390
to whoever he's talking to.
And it has to be exact.

00:02:20.474 --> 00:02:22.643
- Contact now bears
point 0-1-2.

00:02:22.726 --> 00:02:24.770
- Contact...
- 1000 yards and closing.

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:26.855
(sneezes)

00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:28.857
- You gonna do that again?
- No, sir.

00:02:28.941 --> 00:02:30.567
- If so, you will be relieved.

00:02:30.651 --> 00:02:31.401
- Aye, aye, sir.

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:35.405
- Ideally, a convoy
goes across the ocean

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:38.116
without ever talking to anybody
but themselves.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:42.663
Any radio signal going from
the convoy to headquarters,

00:02:42.746 --> 00:02:45.499
you didn't want to send those out,
because if anybody was listening,

00:02:45.582 --> 00:02:46.959
they'd know where you were.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:50.671
- Do I break radio silence
with a message to the admiralty?

00:02:50.754 --> 00:02:52.047
- Every ship was on their own.

00:02:52.589 --> 00:02:55.384
- We are running down a target.
Let us attend our duties well.

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:56.927
This is what we trained for.

00:02:58.262 --> 00:03:00.722
There have been movies
about World War II

00:03:00.806 --> 00:03:03.600
but we expanded it,
embracing the details,

00:03:03.684 --> 00:03:07.813
but at the same time,
tying those details into the human story:

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.440
what do we rely on in
order to get us through this?

00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:13.610
- We'll bring hell down
from on high.

00:03:14.403 --> 00:03:16.947
- Our movie puts
a magnifying glass

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.575
on the history of
the battle of the Atlantic.

00:03:19.658 --> 00:03:20.951
- Here they come.

